 » Abstract  
Stiff limb syndrome (SLS) is a rare "focal" variant of stiff person syndrome which
presents with rigidity and painful spasms of a distal limb, and abnormal fixed foot or
hand postures. Anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase antibodies (GAD-Ab) are variably
present in most cases. Most reported cases of SLS are unassociated with cancer. We
describe a patient with SLS as a paraneoplastic manifestation of breast carcinoma, in
whom GAD-Ab was present. The patient responded very well to oral diazepam,
baclofen and steroids.This is the third reported case of SLS as a paraneoplastic
accompaniment to cancer.
Keywords: Glutamic acid decarboxylase antibody, paraneoplastic, stiff limb syndrome
