 » Introduction  
Stiff limb syndrome (SLS) is a rare "focal" variant of the stiff person syndrome (SPS)
and presents with rigidity and painful spasms of a distal limb, and abnormal fixed foot
or hand postures with sparing of trunk muscles. [1],[2] Most reported cases were not
associated with cancer, and glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD)-antibodies (Ab) are
variably present. [1] We describe a patient with SLS as a paraneoplastic manifestation
of carcinoma of the breast, in whom GAD-Ab were positive. This is the third reported
case of SLS occurring as a paraneoplastic accompaniment to cancer.
 » Case Report  
<seg id="s0001" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">A 55-year-old lady presented with a two-month history of stiffness of right leg and
painful spasms of right foot. She initially noticed an ache in the foot, followed by
difficulty in walking due to stiffness in the leg. It was followed two weeks later by
involuntary abnormal posturing of the right foot and toes. A month later, she
experienced intermittent, painful spasms of the leg during which her foot would be
plantar flexed and inverted for several minutes, making it impossible for her to stand
or walk. Frequent spasms disturbed her sleep. She had lost five kilograms of weight in
the last six months. Past medical history was unremarkable. Family history was
remarkable for esophageal carcinoma in her mother and breast carcinoma in her</seg>
